# Self-signed/local-CA TLS — see docker-compose.with-local-tls.yml's own
# comment for when to reach for this instead of the plain Caddyfile
# (which requests a real Let's Encrypt certificate and needs a public,
# internet-reachable DOMAIN). This binds every interface on 80/443
# rather than one specific hostname, so it works whichever way you
# actually reach this box — by LAN IP, a private hostname, or
# "localhost" — because `tls internal` issues a certificate from
# Caddy's own CA for whatever host the client asked for, rather than
# trying (and failing) to get one from a public CA that's never heard
# of it.
#
# `on_demand` is required here, not optional: the :443 block below has
# no fixed hostname in its site address (that's the whole point — it
# needs to match whatever host the client actually used), and without
# on_demand, Caddy has no hostname to issue a certificate for at config
# load time, so it presents no certificate at all — a hard TLS handshake
# failure (ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR), not a trust warning. This does NOT
# need a global on_demand_tls/ask policy the way public ACME issuers do:
# that requirement exists to stop abuse of rate-limited external CAs,
# and doesn't apply to the internal issuer used here.
#
# Browsers will show the usual self-signed-certificate warning until
# Caddy's root CA is trusted once — that's expected, not a fault. To
# trust it:
#   docker compose exec caddy cat /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt > architecturetoolkit-local-ca.crt
# then import architecturetoolkit-local-ca.crt into your OS's or
# browser's trusted root store. It's the same root every time this
# stack starts — caddy-data is a persistent volume — so this is a
# one-time step per client device, not a per-restart one.
:80 {
	redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}

:443 {
	tls internal {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8080
}
